Martin Read <mpr...@chiark.greenend.org.uk> wrote:

> Training your pet to steal from a shop consumes a
> resource (treats) and lets you get at a bunch of
> already-created resources more easily.

And yet you can steal from a shop, and in particular
have your pet carry out gold dropped on the floor,
(or anything else on the floor, but stolen gold is
the nut of credit cloning) _without_ giving your pet
treats, consuming no resources at all BUT player
patience and game turns.

Being a patient type, I never (any more) waste
treats training pets (they're too valuable for
_taming_ pets); the pet will bring me what I need if
I persist sufficiently.

> Pudding farming *generates* resources essentially
> _ex nihilo_.

No, it requires the exact same two resources as
treatless pet shoplifting: player patience and game
turns. It is in addition significantly dangerous, or
we wouldn't be seeing complaints about pudding farm
bones files.

Neither of those resources is in any sense "free";
player patience eroded means a game likely lost due
to impatient play later, game turns consumed also
eat spellbook timeouts and nourishment points.

        Granted both the latter can be gained _back_
        fairly directly by pudding farming, you have
        no particular guarantee of a new copy of a
        spellbook, at least, no matter how long you
        pudding farm.

> Hence, they are not really comparable parts of the
> game.

I beg to differ. Your viewpoint on pet shoplifting
was too narrow. Correctly considered, it is very
similar to any other patience play mode.

Treats for pets, to reward them for bringing stuff
to the PC and dropping the stuff on a square next to
the PC, it is worth noticing, is very direct
evidence that the DevTeam _designed in_ shoplifting,
which is very strong evidence in support of Joseph N
Hall when he said (else-tread):

        Before you (or anyone else) conclude that
        pudding farming, or any other gimmick, is
